  "text": "As per the information provided, the water tank fills at a rate of 2 cubic feet per minute. It's also stated that the tank's initial empty volume is empty since \"the tank is initially empty.\" However, we need to determine how long it takes to fill the entire tank of 50 cubic feet (10 feet height x 3.14 x 4^2 feet radius x 10 feet height) considering the leaking situation. \n\nAt 0.5 cubic feet per minute (2 cubic feet per minute fill rate - 1 cubic foot per minute leak rate between 0-5 feet), the tank reaches the middle of it, around the 5 to 10 minute mark. At 1 cubic foot per minute (full leak rate), the tank would get empty in 5 minutes. Our scenario is intermediate.\n\nTime needed for leak to eat its quota (of full tank loss in 10 minutes) = 10 minutes x (5/10) = 5 minutes.\n\nThus, the tank will take an additional 5 minutes to fill completely.\n\nत\nAnswer:\n囿This is the Google translation of the problem given in the picture.\n\n囿The given problem can be rephrased as: A tank is being filled with water at a constant rate. There is a hole in the tank that is leaking water at a rate proportional to the square root of the water's depth. If the tank is initially empty and the water level rises to 25 feet after 10 minutes, calculate the total time it takes to fill the entire tank.\n\nLet x and y be the rates of filling and leaking water respectively. \n\nWe can use the relationships:\n\n① y = cx√P\n② v = x - y\n③ e = pt\n\nwhere,\n\n① P = 25 ft is the tank's water depth at 10 min,\n② t = 10 min is the time taken for the water to reach 25 ft,\n③ e = x*10 - y*10 = e\n\nNow solving for y:\n\ny = cx√P\nc = (y/P)/(√P)\nx = (e + (y/P)/(√P))/(10)\n\nTo solve for x and y, stating x > y would be the solution.",
